{"id":"aebe71c3-2f7d-4c03-b65d-d972d5a96bc8","object":"chat.completion","created":1784885644,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    if len(nodes) <= 1:\\n        return {'mst_edges': [], 'total_weight': 0.0}\\n    core = case['core_distance']\\n    distances = case['distances']\\n    edges = []\\n    for u, v, d in distances:\\n        if u > v:\\n            u, v = v, u\\n        mu = max(core[u], core[v], d)\\n        edges.append((mu, u, v))\\n    edges.sort(key=lambda x: (x[0], x[1], x[2]))\\n    parent = {n: n for n in nodes}\\n    rank = {n: 0 for n in nodes}\\n    def find(x):\\n        while parent[x] != x:\\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\\n            x = parent[x]\\n        return x\\n    def union(x, y):\\n        rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\\n        if rx == ry:\\n            return False\\n        if rank[rx] < rank[ry]:\\n            parent[rx] = ry\\n        elif rank[rx] > rank[ry]:\\n            parent[ry] = rx\\n        else:\\n            parent[ry] = rx\\n            rank[rx] += 1\\n        return True\\n    mst_edges = []\\n    total_weight = 0.0\\n    for weight, u, v in edges:\\n        if union(u, v):\\n            mst_edges.append([u, v, weight])\\n            total_weight += weight\\n            if len(mst_edges) == len(nodes) - 1:\\n                break\\n    else:\\n        raise ValueError('Disconnected graph')\\n    return {'mst_edges': mst_edges, 'total_weight': total_weight}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write solve(case) that computes MST of mutual reachability graph. Input case has nodes list, core_distance dict, distances list of [node1, node2, distance]. We need to compute mutual reachability for each pair: max(core[node1], core[node2], distance). Then we need to compute MST using Kruskal's algorithm with deterministic tie-breaking. Tie-breaking: sort by weight, then by endpoint IDs (presumably lexicographically, since node names are strings like \"p0\"). The MST edges must be returned as list of [node1, node2, weight] (with canonical ordering: probably smaller string first). Total weight sum. Must raise ValueError for disconnected graph? The objective says \"Reject disconnected inputs\". So if after processing edges, we cannot form spanning tree (i.e., graph not fully connected), raise ValueError.\n\nImplementation in Python standard library only. Use union-find (DisjointSet). The distances list may not include all pairs? But we need to consider all unordered pairs? The distances list likely includes all pairs (given the public fixtures). But we should compute mutual reachability for each pair present in distances. If graph is not complete, we still need to ensure connectivity; if not connected, raise ValueError.\n\nOutput schema: mst_edges is array of arrays each of length 3: [string, string, number]. total_weight number.\n\nWe must return exactly that JSON object. Must not mutate case.\n\nEdge canonicalization: order endpoints alphabetically or compare strings.\n\nDeterministic union-find: tie-breaking when multiple edges have same weight: sort by weight, then by u, then by v (lexicographically). Union-find should also be deterministic; we don't need special tie-breaking beyond sorting.\n\nWrite solve(case) inside the implementation JSON string.\n\nMake sure to handle floating point numbers; use float comparisons.\n\nLet's code.\n\nWe'll define solve(case): extract nodes, core_distances, distances. Build list of edges with mutual reachability weight. Sort by (weight, u, v). Initialize DSU for nodes. Iterate, for each edge if u and v not connected, add to mst and add weight. Stop when mst has len(nodes)-1 edges. If not, raise ValueError. Return dict.\n\nEdge canonicalization: ensure u <= v (string compare). But note: distances in input may already be unordered? They seem to have both orders? In fixtures, they are like [p0, p1, ...], but they might be ordered arbitrarily. We should canonicalize: if u > v, swap. But need to also ensure that when we output, we output canonical order. The output schema doesn't specify order but to be deterministic we should output u <= v.\n\nAlso note: The distances list might contain both directions? We assume it's one per pair. We'll trust it's unique. If duplicates, we'd need to handle, but unlikely.\n\nCompute core_distance per node from core_distance dict.\n\nNow write the Python code.\n\nWe must output entire response as JSON with key \"implementation\" and value as string containing Python source. So we need to JSON-encode the Python source string, escaping backslashes, quotes, newlines. We'll write the Python code as a multi-line string, then use json.dumps to encode it.\n\nBut careful: The response format requires exactly one JSON object with key implementation, value string. So we produce that.\n\nI'll write:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    # ... \\n    return ...\"}\n\nBut ensure proper escaping. I'll construct manually or use Python script mentally.\n\nLet me write the code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    core = case['core_distance']\n    distances = case['distances']\n    \n    # canonicalize edges and compute mutual reachability\n    edges = []\n    for u, v, d in distances:\n        if u > v:\n            u, v = v, u\n        mu = max(core[u], core[v], d)\n        edges.append((mu, u, v))\n    \n    # sort by weight, then u, then v\n    edges.sort(key=lambda x: (x[0], x[1], x[2]))\n    \n    # union-find\n    parent = {n: n for n in nodes}\n    rank = {n: 0 for n in nodes}\n    \n    def find(x):\n        while parent[x] != x:\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\n            x = parent[x]\n        return x\n    \n    def union(x, y):\n        rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\n        if rx == ry:\n            return False\n        if rank[rx] < rank[ry]:\n            parent[rx] = ry\n        elif rank[rx] > rank[ry]:\n            parent[ry] = rx\n        else:\n            parent[ry] = rx\n            rank[rx] += 1\n        return True\n    \n    mst_edges = []\n    total_weight = 0.0\n    for weight, u, v in edges:\n        if union(u, v):\n            mst_edges.append([u, v, weight])\n            total_weight += weight\n            if len(mst_edges) == len(nodes) - 1:\n                break\n    else:\n        # did not reach n-1 edges\n        raise ValueError(\"Disconnected graph\")\n    \n    return {\"mst_edges\": mst_edges, \"total_weight\": total_weight}\n\nThat's it. Note: the case may have _case_nonce which we ignore. Only use required fields.\n\nNow we need to produce the JSON response. We'll create a Python string of this code, and then embed into JSON. But to avoid manual escaping, I'll write the code as a raw triple-quoted string and then use json.dumps in my mind. However, since I'm outputting text, I need to produce the JSON directly. I'll manually escape: replace newlines with \\n, backslashes with \\\\, quotes with \\\". But careful: The code contains double quotes (strings) and single quotes (inside f-strings?). Actually the code uses single quotes for dict keys, and double quotes? In my code I used single quotes for keys, but also double quotes for f-strings? I used none. I'll rewrite with consistent single quotes to avoid escaping double quotes. But JSON requires double quotes for keys, but inside the Python source string, we can use either. To simplify, I'll use double quotes in Python source for strings? Actually Python source can have both. But when embedding in JSON string, we need to escape double quotes inside the string. If I use single quotes in Python code, then no need to escape double quotes; but JSON string delimiter is double quote, so single quotes are fine. However, the Python source may contain single quotes, which are fine. But to be safe, use double quotes only for the outer JSON, and inside Python code use single quotes. I'll do that.\n\nWrite code with single quotes for dict keys and strings. Also note: we need to ensure we don't have backslashes except for newline. I'll write the code as:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    core = case['core_distance']\n    distances = case['distances']\n    edges = []\n    for u, v, d in distances:\n        if u > v:\n            u, v = v, u\n        mu = max(core[u], core[v], d)\n        edges.append((mu, u, v))\n    edges.sort(key=lambda x: (x[0], x[1], x[2]))\n    parent = {n: n for n in nodes}\n    rank = {n: 0 for n in nodes}\n    def find(x):\n        while parent[x] != x:\n            parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]\n            x = parent[x]\n        return x\n    def union(x, y):\n        rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\n        if rx == ry:\n            return False\n        if rank[rx] < rank[ry]:\n            parent[rx] = ry\n        elif rank[rx] > rank[ry]:\n            parent[ry] = rx\n        else:\n            parent[ry] = rx\n            rank[rx] += 1\n        return True\n    mst_edges = []\n    total_weight = 0.0\n    for weight, u, v in edges:\n        if union(u, v):\n            mst_edges.append([u, v, weight])\n            total_weight += weight\n            if len(mst_edges) == len(nodes) - 1:\n                break\n    else:\n        raise ValueError('Disconnected graph')\n    return {'mst_edges': mst_edges, 'total_weight': total_weight}\n\nNow encode as JSON string: replace newlines with \\n, and escape any backslashes (none) and quotes (single quotes are fine, but double quotes need escape - none). Also note the Python source itself has backslashes? None. So we just need to put \\n between lines. However, we must also escape any embedded double quotes. But we have none. So we can write the entire code as a single line with \\n. Alternatively, we can use a block scalar in JSON? No, JSON string must be escaped newlines.\n\nI'll produce: \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    ...\" etc.\n\nTo make it 